---
// Privacy policy — honest draft grounded in actual data practices:
// this static site collects nothing; the app's practices follow the master
// plan's privacy rules and the shipped account/deletion features.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { appUrl, siteName } from '../../lib/site.js';
---

<Base
	current=""
	title="Privacy"
	description="Draft privacy policy: what this static site collects (nothing) and what the application records."
>
	<article class="legal">
		<h1>Privacy</h1>
		<p class="draft-banner small">
			Draft for owner review — pending legal sign-off. It describes current
			behavior, not aspirations.
		</p>

		<h2>1. This website</h2>
		<p>
			This marketing site is static. It holds no learner data, sets no
			tracking or advertising cookies, and runs no analytics. It links to
			the application; it never receives your data.
		</p>

		<h2>2. What the application records</h2>
		<p>
			Your account (email and sign-in method, including institutional
			single sign-on where configured), your registered devices and
			sessions, your study evidence — answers, confidence, declared
			assistance, elapsed time — your plans and goals, your notes and
			question marks, and any private imports you upload.
		</p>

		<h2>3. What stays yours alone</h2>
		<p>
			Notes, question marks, and private imports are readable only by your
			account. Private imports may not contain patient-identifiable
			information and are readable only while licensing permits. Assisted
			attempts are excluded from community statistics.
		</p>

		<h2>4. Institutions</h2>
		<p>
			Where your account is enrolled by an institution, institution staff
			can see program-level progress within policy — never your private
			notes, imports, or private feedback beyond what policy grants.
			Access is role-scoped and audited.
		</p>

		<h2>5. Deletion</h2>
		<p>
			You can delete your account from the application. Deletion is
			immediate for active sessions and propagates through your stored
			data under the retention policy documented with the service.
		</p>

		<h2>6. AI assistance</h2>
		<p>
			Tutoring features are deterministic or run under licensed, audited
			model providers; prompts carry your study context, not your
			identity, and tutoring that uses your data is disclosed in the
			product where it happens.
		</p>

		<h2>7. Changes</h2>
		<p>
			This policy will be updated before any material change in data
			practices takes effect — including the opening of payments — and the
			service will surface significant changes in the application.
		</p>

		<p class="muted small">
			Privacy questions while the service is in development: use the
			in-app channels — <a href={appUrl}>open the app</a>.
		</p>
	</article>
</Base>
